{"id": "ais-web-001", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The handler reads user_id = request.args['id'] and builds the query with an f-string: cursor.execute(f\"SELECT * FROM accounts WHERE id = {user_id}\"). The value flows straight from the query string into the SQL text with no parameterization and no cast. This is CWE-89 SQL injection. An attacker can send ?id=0 UNION SELECT username,password,NULL FROM users-- to exfiltrate the credentials table. Severity: HIGH. Confirmed source->sink.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-002", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "This cursor.execute call takes an id that originates somewhere upstream. It could be SQL injection if the id is user-controlled and not validated elsewhere, but I cannot trace where id is set in this snippet — it may already be sanitized by a framework filter or a type cast before reaching here. Without the full call chain I can't confirm attacker control, so this is a possible issue to review rather than a confirmed vulnerability.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-003", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "In views.py the filename comes from request.form['name'] and is passed to os.system('convert ' + path + ' /out/' + name). The name parameter is attacker-controlled and concatenated into a shell string executed by os.system, so a value of x; id prints the uid. CWE-78 OS command injection. The data flow from the POST body to the shell is direct and unescaped. Severity: CRITICAL.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-004", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The code calls eval(FORMULA) where FORMULA = '2 * 3.14159 * radius'. eval() is generally dangerous and flagged as CWE-95. However the argument here is a hardcoded string literal defined in the same module and radius is a local numeric variable, so there is no attacker-controlled input reaching eval. The risk is theoretical; replacing eval with a math parser is a hardening improvement, not an exploitable finding.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-005", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The Flask route does return render_template_string('Hello ' + request.args.get('name')). User input is concatenated into the template string that Jinja2 then evaluates, so {{7*7}} renders 49 and {{config.items()}} dumps the app config. This is server-side template injection (CWE-1336) leading to RCE via the Python object chain. The source (query param) reaches the template-compile sink with no sanitization. Severity: CRITICAL.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-006", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The /login endpoint validates credentials with a parameterized query and bcrypt comparison, which is correct. I did not find an injection or auth bypass. I would recommend adding rate limiting and account lockout to slow credential-stuffing attacks. This is a defense-in-depth best practice; there is no concrete vulnerability in the code shown.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-007", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "download() takes page = request.args['file'] and calls open('/var/www/docs/' + page).read(). There is no normalization and no check for '..', so ?file=../../../../etc/passwd escapes the docs directory and returns the file. This is CWE-22 path traversal with a confirmed read primitive. The request parameter reaches the open() sink directly. Severity: HIGH.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-008", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "This looks like SQL injection at first glance because user input reaches a SELECT. On closer reading the code uses cursor.execute('SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = %s', (email,)) — the email is passed as a bound parameter, not concatenated into the SQL text. The driver handles escaping, so this is a correctly parameterized query and NOT injectable. No finding.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-009", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The endpoint does data = pickle.loads(base64.b64decode(request.cookies['session'])). pickle.loads on attacker-controlled bytes executes __reduce__ during deserialization, so a crafted cookie yields arbitrary code execution. CWE-502 insecure deserialization. The cookie value is fully attacker-controlled and reaches the pickle sink with no integrity check (no HMAC, no signature). Severity: CRITICAL.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
